{
  "header": [
    "UNALLOCATED — ⚖ DISPUTED — is an authenticate whose peer_id is a valid NON-canonical form of the signing key refused 401 identity_mismatch, or accepted?",
    "",
    "Batch 4. §4.6 step 3 derives the expected peer_id with §7.4, which constructs canonical form only; §1.5's",
    "wire-acceptance carve-out says a non-canonical form MAY be decoded and refusal is a deployment's stricter",
    "policy. The same input is a MUST-refuse under one and a permitted accept under the other. F31."
  ],
  "id_status": "unallocated",
  "under": "",
  "title": "An authenticate whose peer_id (and hello.peer_id) is the SHA-256 form of the Ed25519 key it is signed with is refused 401 identity_mismatch",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §4.6 step 3 ('The responder MUST verify that `authenticate.peer_id` is the peer-id derived from `authenticate.public_key` (per the §1.5 / §7.4 construction `Base58(key_type ‖ hash_type ‖ hash(public_key))`). A mismatch MUST be rejected with status 401 `identity_mismatch`.')",
  "spec_also": [
    "§1.5 wire-acceptance carve-out ('Implementations MAY decode `peer_id`s presented in non-canonical form ... Impls MAY refuse non-canonical form at construction or handshake under stricter local policy (deployment choice).') — CONTRADICTS",
    "§7.4 ('MUST construct using canonical `hash_type`'; the SHA-256 form for Ed25519 'is the §1.5 wire-acceptance carve-out (decode-only for backwards compat)')",
    "§4.6 ('The responder MUST also verify `authenticate.peer_id == hello.peer_id`')",
    "§4.7 (a MUST-emit code table — no code for a policy refusal of a non-canonical form)",
    "§1.4 peer reference formula ('Base58(key_type || hash_type || hash(pubkey))') — wrong for identity-multihash"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "disputed",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F31",
  "reading": [
    "READING R — REFUSE, 401 identity_mismatch. Step 3 says the peer_id must be \"the peer-id derived from",
    "`authenticate.public_key` (per the §1.5 / §7.4 construction\". §7.4 is the derivation and it has one output:",
    "`hash_type = canonical_hash_type(key_type)`. A SHA-256-form string is not that output, so it is \"a mismatch\",",
    "and \"A mismatch MUST be rejected with status 401 `identity_mismatch`\". The responder has everything it needs:",
    "the key is in the same message. Under R the carve-out's MAY-decode governs peer_ids met ELSEWHERE (paths,",
    "patterns, gossip), never the one being bound to a key.",
    "",
    "READING A — ACCEPT (and canonicalize), with refusal a deployment option. §1.5: \"Implementations MAY decode",
    "`peer_id`s presented in non-canonical form ... for backwards compatibility with older implementations.",
    "Implementations MUST canonicalize on acceptance ... derive `(public_key, key_type)` from the presented form",
    "(post-handshake when pubkey is available ...)\". And explicitly: \"Impls MAY refuse non-canonical form at",
    "construction or handshake under stricter local policy\". If refusing at handshake is a STRICTER local policy,",
    "accepting at handshake is the default one — which R makes impossible. And step 3's own formula is written",
    "with `hash_type` free (\"key_type ‖ hash_type ‖ hash(public_key)\"), which reads as \"consistent with the",
    "presented hash_type\", not \"equal to the canonical string\".",
    "",
    "WHY WE DO NOT PICK. Both readings quote a keyword over the same input and neither sentence names the other.",
    "The proposal the carve-out came from (v7.65 §5.1) scopes acceptance to \"pre-v7.64 cohorts ... still rotating",
    "to canonical form\" and \"MAY refuse ... at construction or handshake\", which favours A — but a proposal is not",
    "the specification (prohibition 2), and §4.6 step 3 was rewritten later (0.8.1, 0.8.2.4) without touching the",
    "carve-out. Real degrees of freedom.",
    "",
    "WHAT BOTH READINGS AGREE ON, AND IS THEREFORE NOT DISPUTED, AND SCORED ELSEWHERE:",
    "  - a SHA-256-form string whose digest is NOT SHA-256 of the signing key never completes a handshake (a",
    "    mismatch under both; a strict policy may refuse it earlier, with an unpinned code), and a canonical-form",
    "    string for a different key is 401 `identity_mismatch` under every reading and policy (the control below;",
    "    `ECP-R51`, connect-time proof-of-possession, is the floor row, and is not authored in this batch);",
    "  - under A, an accepted handshake binds the CANONICAL identity: grantee invariance",
    "    (`UNALLOCATED-system-peer-hashable-basis-excludes-peer-id`) and canonical lookup",
    "    (`UNALLOCATED-accepted-non-canonical-peer-id-canonicalized-before-use`).",
    "",
    "A GAP UNDER A, ROUTED WITH THE DISPUTE. A stricter-policy refusal has no code. §4.7 is \"a normative MUST-emit",
    "contract\" and lists none for it; a peer refusing with 401 identity_mismatch, 400 invalid_request, or a close",
    "would each be defensible. If A is ruled, the code must be too.",
    "",
    "WORDING, ROUTED WITH THE DISPUTE. §4.6 step 3 and §1.4 (and, by review, §2.8, §5.4's `is_peer_id` comment and",
    "ENTITY-NATIVE-TYPE-SYSTEM §4.8, as \"Ed25519 + SHA-256\") all write the construction as",
    "`Base58(key_type ‖ hash_type ‖ hash(public_key))`. For the canonical Ed25519 form \"the digest IS the public",
    "key\" — there is no hash. The formula is the pre-v7.64 construction and should cite §7.4's pseudocode instead."
  ],
  "dispute": {
    "question": "Is an authenticate whose peer_id is a valid non-canonical form of its own public key an identity_mismatch (§4.6 step 3 via §7.4) or an acceptable presentation that the responder canonicalizes (§1.5 carve-out) — and if accepted by default, what code does a stricter-policy refusal emit?",
    "why_it_matters": "a client still presenting the SHA-256 form connects to an A peer and is refused 401 by an R peer. Every downstream canonicalization requirement (lookup, policy, grantee invariance) is reachable over the handshake only under A.",
    "owner": "entity-system-architecture",
    "our_lean": "none recorded. Under R, §1.5's 'MAY refuse ... at handshake' is vacuous; under A, step 3's 'derived ... per §7.4' is not literally a derivation. Both readings make one sentence do less than it says.",
    "blocked_work": "this requirement; the handshake members of the two files named in `reading`.",
    "suite_reports": "SKIP — disputed, naming this question. The witness arm runs and records outcome and code."
  },
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [],
    "identities": [
      "caller"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy",
      "close_wait_bound"
    ]
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "reading-R-refused",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "hello.peer_id = authenticate.peer_id = Base58(0x01 0x01 || SHA-256(P)); authenticate.public_key = P, signed with K; all other steps valid",
      "why": "conformant under R only. NOT SCORED while disputed.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "capture": "sha256_form",
          "peer_id_form": "sha256-form"
        }
      ],
      "witness": [
        {
          "field": "outcome_status_and_code",
          "why": "the cohort's split is the most useful input to the ruling."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "reading-A-accepted",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "the same input",
      "why": "conformant under A only: the handshake completes. NOT SCORED while disputed.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "capture": "sha256_form_a",
          "peer_id_form": "sha256-form"
        }
      ],
      "witness": [
        {
          "field": "outcome_status_and_code",
          "why": "same observation, recorded against the other reading."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "two scored probes, each on a fresh connection, public_key = P and signed with K: (i) hello.peer_id = authenticate.peer_id = C(P'), the CANONICAL form of a different key P'; (ii) the same with S(P'), the SHA-256 form of the different key",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY, and scored now, because both are wrong under every reading AND every local policy. (i) is a plain identity mismatch in canonical form: §4.6 step 3 → 401 `identity_mismatch`, which no stricter policy changes. (ii) must merely NOT COMPLETE: under R it is a mismatch; under A the digest is not SHA-256(P), so it is a mismatch; and a strict peer may refuse the form earlier with a code §4.7 does not pin — so (ii) asserts refusal, never a code. ⚠ Corrected after review: the first draft scored (ii) as 401 `identity_mismatch`, which a strict-policy peer refusing at hello would fail while conformant.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "capture": "wrong_key_canonical",
          "peer_id_form": "canonical-of-other-key"
        },
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "capture": "wrong_key_sha256_form",
          "peer_id_form": "sha256-form-of-other-key"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "wrong_key_canonical",
          "equals": 401
        },
        {
          "kind": "error_code",
          "capture": "wrong_key_canonical",
          "equals": "identity_mismatch"
        }
      ],
      "refuse": [
        {
          "outcome": "handshake_completes",
          "capture": "wrong_key_sha256_form",
          "why": "the one outcome no reading permits: a SHA-256-form string bound to a key it is not the hash of."
        }
      ],
      "witness": [
        {
          "field": "outcome_status_and_code",
          "why": "which step refused it, and with what code — input to the F31 code question."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer that treats any decodable Base58 peer_id as bound to any key",
      "expect": "control probe (ii) completes the handshake — it decodes the SHA-256 form and never compares the digest",
      "why": "the §4.6 step-3 hazard: 'an unbound proof lets a caller act under another peer's identity'. Non-conformant under both readings."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "peer_canonicalization/peer_canon_2_canonicalize_on_storage",
    "oracle_note": "provenance only. The check's citation says 'non-canonical wire form decodes' — it asserts reading A. Whether it presents the form at the handshake or elsewhere is not known from its citation."
